.TH SCO_X286 4 "2026-09-04" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
sco_x286 \- SCO Xenix/286 execution personality
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B SCO-X/286
.RB ( PERS_SCO_X286 )
runs SCO Xenix/286 binaries \(em 16-bit protected-mode System V.2 programs in
the segmented
.B x.out
format described in
.BR xout286 (4).
.PP
The x.out world is not one personality.  Each vendor/processor pairing is a
distinct ABI: the 8086 and 80286 images are 16-bit segmented and trap through
.BR "int $5" ,
while the 80386 ones are 32-bit and use the System V
.B "lcall $7,$0"
gate, and the Microsoft-branded releases predate SCO's and differ again.
Substrate reserves a separate personality id for each; this page covers the
SCO 80286 one.
.SH SYSTEM CALL INTERFACE
A Xenix/286 program traps with
.BR "int $5" .
Substrate leaves IDT vector 5 at DPL 0, so the instruction faults with
.B #GP
before it can reach the
.B #BR
handler; the personality catches that fault, confirms the faulting bytes are
.BR "CD 05" ,
and emulates the call.
.PP
The register convention, taken from the Xenix crt0's
.B __syscall
thunk at text offset 2, is:
.TP
.B AX
Call number.  The low byte is the System V call; the high byte is a
sub-function, used by call 40 (the Xenix multiplexer, which carries
.BR brkctl ,
.BR ftime ,
.BR nap ,
.BR locking ,
.BR rdchk ,
.BR chsize ,
the shared-data and IPC calls) and by call 57
.RB ( utssys :
0 is
.BR uname ,
2 is
.BR ustat ).
.TP
.BR BX ", " CX ", " SI ", " DI
Arguments one through four, one word each.  A long is split across two
consecutive slots, low half first.
.TP
.B AX
On return, the result \(em or, when the carry flag is set, the positive
.BR errno .
.TP
.B BX
On return, the high half of a 32-bit result.  The Xenix stubs move it to
.B DX
to build the
.B DX:AX
that Microsoft C returns longs in.  Calls that yield two values use the same
pair:
.B getpid
returns pid in AX and ppid in BX,
.B getuid
returns uid and euid,
.B pipe
returns both descriptors, and
.B wait
returns the pid in AX and the status in BX.
.PP
.BR fork (2)
is the exception to the AX rule: it returns the
.I other
process's pid in AX, and the child is told apart by
.B BX
being zero.  The child does not re-enter the trap handler, so its BX is
staged into the trap frame before the fork and the parent's is restored
after.
.SH TRANSLATED INTERFACES
.IP \(bu 2
.BR open (2)
flags.  Xenix uses the System V values \(em
.B O_NDELAY
is 0004,
.B O_CREAT
0400,
.B O_TRUNC
01000,
.B O_EXCL
02000 \(em where Substrate uses the Linux ones.  The same translation applies
to
.B fcntl(F_GETFL)
and
.BR F_SETFL .
.IP \(bu
.BR ioctl (2).
The Xenix termio group
.RB ( TCGETA
= 0x5401) sits one lower than Substrate's, which reserves 0x5401\(en0x5404
for the termios family Xenix has no equivalent of.  The 17-byte
.B struct termio
is converted to and from
.BR "struct termios" ,
including the System V aliasing of
.B VMIN
and
.B VTIME
onto
.B VEOF
and
.BR VEOL .
.IP \(bu
.BR stat (2)
and
.BR fstat (2)
report the 30-byte Xenix
.B struct stat
(16-bit device, inode, mode, link count, uid, gid and rdev; 32-bit size and
times).
.IP \(bu
.BR read (2)
on a directory.  Xenix/286 has no
.BR getdents (2):
a directory is read with
.BR read (2)
and returns 16-byte V7
.B struct direct
records.  Substrate synthesizes them, which is what makes
.B ls
list a directory and
.BR ttyname (3)
find the terminal by scanning
.IR /dev .
.IP \(bu
Signal numbers.  Xenix and Substrate both descend from V7 but extended it
differently above SIGTERM, and at 7 and 10.
.IP \(bu
.BR brkctl (2),
the segmented
.BR sbrk (2).
DGROUP's break is the process break; a far data segment carries its break in
its own descriptor limit, and
.B BR_NEWSEG
allocates a fresh LDT slot with a 64 KiB window behind it.
.SH SIGNALS
Delivery is V7 style, which is what Xenix/286 is.  The handler's far pointer
is pushed as a far-call frame on the program's own 16-bit stack:
.IP
.nf
SP+4   signal number   (the handler's int argument)
SP+2   interrupted CS  \\  the return address, so the handler's
SP+0   interrupted IP  /  lret resumes the interrupted instruction
.fi
.PP
There is no trampoline and no saved register block, because V7 had none: the
handler is an ordinary C function, so it preserves what the ABI requires and
everything else is understood to be clobbered.  The disposition reverts to
.B SIG_DFL
as the handler is entered, and the handler re-arms it.
.SH FILESYSTEM NAMESPACE
The personality's path prefix is
.IR /perso/xenix286s .
An absolute path is resolved there first and then against the root
filesystem, so a Xenix
.I /perso/xenix286s/bin/sh
wins over the native one.
.PP
For everything except
.BR execve (2),
the bare-path fallback refuses Substrate-native
.RB ( ELFOSABI_SUBSTRATE )
objects, because pulling one into a process that stays Xenix would have its
Substrate-numbered syscalls misrouted by this personality.
.BR execve (2)
is exempt: it replaces the image outright and the loader that claims the new
file sets the personality with it, so a Xenix program can exec a Substrate
binary and simply becomes a native process.  Both directions work \(em
Microsoft Word execs
.I /bin/sh
into Substrate's shell, which execs a Xenix
.I SetWrdKeyMap
straight back into this personality.
.SH DEBUGGING
.TP
.B debug=perso:x286:xout
Trace image loading: processor, run-time flags, entry segment, segment count,
and the selectors and offsets the process is entered with.
.TP
.B debug=perso:x286:syscall
Trace each emulated call as it returns, with its arguments and result.
.TP
.B debug=perso:x286:entry
Also trace each call on the way in.  This is what identifies a program parked
in a blocking call, which the return-side trace cannot show.
.SH KNOWN LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
The shared-data
.RB ( sdget ,
.BR sdenter ,
.BR sdleave ,
.BR sdgetv ,
.BR sdwaitv ),
semaphore
.RB ( creatsem ,
.BR waitsem ,
.BR sigsem )
and System V IPC sub-functions of call 40 return
.BR ENOSYS .
.IP \(bu
.BR fcntl (2)
record locking
.RB ( F_GETLK ,
.BR F_SETLK ,
.BR F_SETLKW )
returns
.BR EINVAL ;
.B struct flock
is not translated.
.IP \(bu
.BR ptrace (2),
.BR profil (2)
and
.BR uadmin (2)
are not implemented.
.IP \(bu
.BR utssys (2)
implements only
.BR uname ;
.B ustat
returns
.BR ENOSYS .
.IP \(bu
System-call pointer arguments are always resolved as near offsets in the
trapping
.BR DS .
Large-data images
.RB ( XE_LDATA )
themselves load and run \(em they are handed
.I argv
and
.I envp
as arrays of far pointers rather than near offsets \(em but a program that
passes a far pointer to a call such as
.BR read (2)
would have only the offset half read.
.SH VERIFIED
SCO Xenix/286
.BR echo ,
.BR cat ,
.B ls -l
(including the V7 directory-read path),
.BR sync ,
.B csh
and
.B vi
(middle model, full screen) run correctly, as does cross-personality
.BR execve (2)
in both directions.  A 37-command sample of the distribution media runs
clean: 37 ok, 0 crash, 0 noload.
.PP
The shipped
.B cc
compiles and links end to end, with correct floating point \(em these
binaries carry no x87 instructions, the linker having rewritten each into a
two-byte software interrupt, and the loader patches them back.  Microsoft
Word 3.0 for Xenix reaches its full editing screen under
.BR TERM=ansi .
.PP
Xenix/86 images
.RB ( x_cpu
0x04) run under this same personality: they are protected-mode images
carrying the same LDT selectors as an 80286 one, and the 8086 instruction
set is a strict subset.
